{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1HnHCHtGja9r9afD8Wk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. I read the statement and I don't have a proof I can formalize in this setting.\n\nThe statement is not trivially true. `P` must have type `Polynomial ℤ`, since `P = Q * R` with `Q R : Polynomial ℤ`, and the solution is `True`. So the goal is the real content of Putnam 2021 A6: if a 0/1-coefficient polynomial factors into nonconstant integer polynomials, then `P(2)` is composite.\n\nTwo of the three conjuncts are routine: `P(2) > 0` because the coefficients are nonnegative and `P ≠ 0`, and `P(2) ≠ 1` because otherwise `P` would be constant. The non-primality is the hard part. The standard argument needs an analytic bound on the roots of 0/1 polynomials (a Mahler-measure-type estimate) to show that a prime value forces one factor to be ±1 at `2`, which is a contradiction. Mathlib has no ready-made tool for that, and I didn't have a route to build it in this session.\n\nI did not modify `/work/putnam_2021_a6.lean`, so the `sorry` remains."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":428,"cache_read_input_tokens":1881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":428,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76f61e21-0c7c-463c-9a87-3a8c5a9fae67","uuid":"1f3101d2-430a-4e26-8c58-2e8ae265f901","timestamp":"2026-10-14T01:50:08.431Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1HnGsgwmaYDAocLVPqr"}
